steps.body:
A `double` can hold infinity. Get it from `<limits>` and flip its sign with unary minus to get negative infinity. Infinity is a handy starting value for "nothing found yet", because every finite number is smaller than it.

```cpp
const double best = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
const double worst = -best;  // negative infinity
```

`std::cout` prints them as `inf` and `-inf`. Arithmetic keeps them infinite: `best + 100` is still `inf`. g++ follows the IEEE 754 floating-point rules, so dividing a nonzero double by `0.0` gives infinity with the matching sign: `1.0 / 0.0` is `inf` and `-1.0 / 0.0` is `-inf`. Integer division by zero is different: `1 / 0` is undefined behavior, so never run it.

To test for infinity, use `std::isinf(x)` from `<cmath>`. Some operations have no sensible answer: `best - best`, `best * 0`, and `0.0 / 0.0` are NaN, "not a number". NaN prints as `nan` or `-nan` depending on how it was made, so never print it. Test it with `std::isnan(x)` instead.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    const double best = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
    {{code}}
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
std::cout << std::boolalpha << std::isinf(best) << '\n';  // true
std::cout << std::isnan(best - best) << '\n';  // true
```

example.code:
#include <cmath>
#include <iostream>
#include <limits>

int main() {
    const double best = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
    const double worst = -best;

    std::cout << best << ' ' << worst << '\n';
    std::cout << best + 100 << ' ' << worst * 2 << ' ' << 1.0 / 0.0 << '\n';

    std::cout << std::boolalpha;
    std::cout << std::isinf(best) << ' ' << std::isinf(42.0) << ' '
              << std::isnan(best - best) << '\n';
    return 0;
}

